;; FH-BEHAVIOR-004 — connection happens at COMMIT, and `:update` observes
;; movement rather than renders.
;;
;; This is the row a structural test cannot reach. A behavior's whole value is
;; that its imperative work is bounded to moments the substrate names, and the
;; first of those moments is the host's own COMMIT — never a render. React
;; renders speculatively: a transition that suspends, a superseded update, a
;; StrictMode double-render. If a behavior connected from a render, every one of
;; those would open a host connection the application never asked for and
;; nothing would ever close.
;;
;; So the probe below drives a genuinely abandoned candidate — the same shape
;; `presence_commit_ownership_dom_cljs_test` uses: the behavior's use site is
;; rendered inside a transition whose sibling suspends, so the candidate really
;; runs and really never commits. The lifecycle transcript must be EMPTY.
;;
;; The second half is `:update`'s trigger. A re-render with `rf=`-equal config is
;; not movement, and a behavior that reconciled its host on every commit would
;; reset a cursor, a selection or a scroll offset for no reason at all.
;;
;; `:timing` is the third: a `:layout` behavior's work must be complete before
;; the browser paints, which is only observable as a DOM fact — the attribute
;; below is present on the node in the same commit that mounted it.
{:fh/id "FH-BEHAVIOR-004"
 :fh/law
 "A behavior connects only from a SELECTED commit — a candidate render the host abandons connects nothing — and `:update` runs only when the committed config MOVES by `rf=`; a `:layout` behavior's work is complete before the browser paints."

 ;; The lifecycle transcript (`{:op …}` in order) after each step of the probe.
 :abandoned    []
 :committed    [:connect]
 :equal-config [:connect]
 :moved-config [:connect :update]

 ;; What the moved-config `:update` must see.
 :moved
 {:config      {:label "moved"}
  :prev-config {:label "a"}
  :memory      {:updates 0}}

 ;; The `:layout` arm, read off the node in the commit that mounted it.
 :layout {:attribute "data-measured" :value "layout"}

 ;; `:mixed-timing` declares the PASSIVE behavior first in document order and
 ;; the LAYOUT behavior second. The connect order below is therefore the
 ;; declared timing overriding document order — which is what "before the
 ;; browser paints" buys, and the one observable form the claim takes.
 :timing-order
 [:re-frame.freehand.behavior-views/measure
  :re-frame.freehand.behavior-views/probe]}
